Z||000006||theogre||11-04-2001||03:03 PM||||No idea why the SES light came on.... read the codes.<P>The clutch is a heat problem of some type.<P>It could be fluid boiling due to engine bay heat. The fluid can boil at quite low temperatures if it's moisture soaked. (read about brake fluid in my cave.... Body and Chasis menu.)<P>completely flush the clutch hydraulics with Valvoline Synthetic brake fluid. This fluid is about the same price as most others but has a higher wet boiling point, so it will be less affected by moisture left in the system after flushing.<P>I'd flush the fluid first. just to eliminate the posibility of that being a problem. If that doesn't get it then you could have probmens inside the bell housing. Hard to say exactly what.<P>I haven't dealt with a hydraulic clutch in a long time, but this is how I used to flush them.<P>1. suck the old fluid from the MC with a turkey baster. Stick a soft rag in there to soak up and clean out sediment. Don't try to get every drop! You don't want to get air into the MC bore... That means leave the well in the tank bottom alone.<P>2. Fill the MC with new fluid.<P>3. While someone else watches the MC, use a vacuum bleeder set to pull the fluid thru the system. KEEP THE MC FULL!!!!! You should see a pronounced color shift when new fluid reaches the slave.<P>to get the last of the old fluid out of the slave more quickly....<P>4. With the bleeder open push the slave piston in SLOWLY. Hold it there and cloase the bleeder. Let the piston out VERY SLOWLY! KEEP THE MC FULL!!!!!<P>5. open the bleeder and repeat step 4 again. KEEP THE MC FULL!!!!!<P>This should remove the vast majority of old fluid and any small air bubbles.<P>When you let the piston out you must do it slowly or air can be sucked past the seal. The slave piston is sprung, so unless the slave cylinder has something wrong it should push the piston out for you.<P>Vacuum bleeder sets are available thru most autoparts stores. Just buy one. You'll need it again for future clutch and brake work.<P>If the vacuum sucks air around the bleeder screw, wrap it with a couple turns of teflon plumbing tape and tread it in again.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
